  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Note that plugging in $x=1$ gives the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}.$ However, we can use L'Hopital's rule to find this limit:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11}{4{x}^{3}-4} & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{[5{x}^{3}+6{x}^{2}-11]^{\u2032}}{[4{x}^{3}-4]^{\u2032}} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{15{x}^{2}+12x}{12{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15(1)^{2}+12(1)}{12(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15+12}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
